    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d962">

                <pb facs="0823" n="815" xml:id="pg815"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.79611 Putnam, Amelia Earhart/141</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum, by Mr. William T. Turner of the Division
                        of Far Eastern Affairs of a Conversation With the Second Secretary of the
                        Japanese Embassy</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Hayama</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-07-06">July 6, 1937</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Mr. Hayama telephoned that the Naval Attaché of the Japanese Embassy had been
                    informed that the Japanese Navy Department had instructed the survey ship <hi
                        rendition="#italic">Koshu</hi>, 2500 tons, which is now somewhere in the
                    South Seas, to take part in the search for the airplane of Miss Earhart and to
                    get into touch with Japanese vessels near the place where Miss Earhart’s
                    airplane is reported to have been lost.</p>
            </div>
